  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 207 PM CDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nHigh pressure centered over the area this afternoon will drift \nsoutheastward through Tuesday, supporting a dry and relatively \nquite period of weather. Patchy shallow fog will be possible \nlate tonight primarily for interior areas south of the Chicago \nmetro and across northwest Indiana. Otherwise, expect another \ncool night tonight with lows in the 50s and mild day Tuesday \nwith highs around 80.\n\nAn upper-level low centered just north of the Boundary Waters \nof Minnesota Tuesday night will track to east of Lake Superior \nby Wednesday evening. A series of weak mid-level impulses will \npinwheel around the low across Wisconsin and Lower Michigan \nduring this time. Meanwhile, mid-level moisture originating from\nthe southwestern CONUS will advect northeast across the mid-\nMississippi River Valley as modestly steeper mid to upper-level \nlapse rates shift over the area. Conditions appear favorable for\nat least some convection over the forecast area during the \nafternoon and early evening, but there are several factors that \ncomplicate both the coverage and severity of that convection. \nThe initial weak waves late Tuesday night through early \nafternoon Wednesday will bring mid-level clouds and possibly \ndecaying high-based showers and embedded thunderstorms, \nresulting in questions with sufficient destabilization even as a\nnarrow ribbon of higher low-level moisture advects in from the \nsouthwest. Forcing may also remain mis-aligned, with the upper-\nlow to the north, tail-end of the mid-level troughs overhead, \nrelatively weak upper-level jet to the south, and trailing cold \nfront to the west. Conditional on erosion of the low-level cap \nand better alignment of the forcing noted above, deep-layer \nshear will be favorable for multicell clusters with embedded \nweak supercell structures, with locally damaging winds the \nprimary threat, mid-afternoon into early evening.\n\nMid-level ridging over the Canadian Prairies will deamplify \nwhile shifting eastward late this week into the weekend. \nAssociated surface high pressure will track east while becoming \nelongated across the central Great Lakes Thursday and Friday. \nCannot fully rule out sprinkles or weak showers on the lake \nbreeze boundary across northeast Illinois late Thursday \nafternoon, but the subsidence inversion should be strong enough \nto stunt any growing diurnal cumulus.\n\nA loosely zonal pattern across North America this weekend into \nearly next week favors a more active storm track north of the \nforecast area, but close enough to warrant periods of low-end \nprecip chances for showers and some thunderstorms as early as \nSaturday night. Summer warmth will also return as we close out \nAugust with increasing humidity and daytime highs pushing well \ninto the 80s (assuming active weather remains bottled up to the \nnorth).\n\nKluber",
